|> Exercise
    handle=_cosine_rule_geometric_1_

    In the figure below, where can

    $$
    \Large ab\cos\theta_{ab}
    $$

    be found, if at all? (Note that “$\theta_{ab}$” is
    just a name.)

    |> Image
        src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_statement.svg

    |> Solution

        It appears two places; it is the area of 
        both of these rectangles:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_sol1_v2.svg

        In more detail, here is how “$b\cdot\cos\theta_{ab}$” 
        and “$a\cdot\cos\theta_{ab}$” show up as the widths of
        the two rectangles, respectively, zooming in:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_sol34_combined.svg

        |> Pause

        Thus the rectangles respectively have areas...

        $$
        \Large a(b\cdot \cos\theta_{ab}) = ab\cos\theta_{ab}
        $$
        ...and...
        $$
        \Large b(a\cdot \cos\theta_{ab}) = ab\cos\theta_{ab}
        $$

        ...as claimed!